- [X] T055 [P] [US7] Wrote `apps/web/tests/rate-limit/fixed-window.test.ts` — 5 tests, all passing against real Redis (key namespace `ratelimit:{randomUUID}:...` can't collide with anything else, safe to run against the shared instance directly). Covers: N-then-rejected; next-window recovery; per-user isolation; per-category isolation (bonus, beyond the original ask); and the fail-open behavior on a genuinely unreachable Redis (bounded-retry connection to a closed port) — confirmed both that the request is allowed *and* that the failure is logged (`RATE_LIMIT_CHECK_FAILED`), not silently swallowed.
- [X] T056 [P] [US7] Wrote `apps/web/tests/rate-limit/route-enforcement.test.ts` — 6 tests against a real rebuilt `next start` server, all passing, covering all 5 routes plus a "fresh user's first request is never blocked" check. **Found and fixed a real test-design bug before it could produce a false pass**: three of the five routes share `category: "import"`, and the rate-limit key is `ratelimit:{userId}:{category}:...` — scoped by category, not by route path. My first draft reused one user/cookie across all three "import" routes, which would have made each test's counter bleed into the next one's and produce misleading counts. Fixed by giving every single test its own freshly-created user. Also confirmed no regression: reran `test:job-queue` (32/32, unchanged), `test:local-folder-import` (19/20 — the same pre-existing, already-disclosed `ownership-matrix.test.ts` flake, not new), and `test:repository-import-request` (10/10) with the rate limiting live — nothing that previously passed now hits 429.

